item.text:
{
  "concepts": [
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The DESIGN_MODULE profile describes Ontology-first engineering design and preferred realisation structure inside implementation and design modules, with explicit authority, effects, low coupling and semantic non-bleed. Its subject Ontology is an engineering model of a designed boundary.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":2463,
          "quote": "this profile what is\n the preferred implementation design discipline",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/DESIGN_MODULE.md#DESIGN_MODULE%2F12.%20Adoption%20and%20relations%2FRelationship%20to%20other%20members",
          "start_line":2459
        }
      ],
      "key": "design_module_profile",
      "label": "Design-module profile"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The Ontology is one semantic truth. Diagrams, matrices, IACS, public interfaces, schemas, architecture, modules, implementation and proof inventories preserve that truth as projections; their presence does not make them independent semantic authorities.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1078,
          "quote": "The Ontology is one semantic truth (P1, P6; rc.2 #ontology-projection-law).\nThese preserve it and do not become independent semantic authorities:",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/DESIGN_MODULE.md#DESIGN_MODULE%2F5.%20Ontology-first%20design%2FOntology%20projection",
          "start_line":1077
        }
      ],
      "key": "ontology",
      "label": "Ontology"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "An atomic function family is a parameterised semantic operation derived from the Ontology to make entity lifecycles logically complete. Its derivation evidence associates discovered functionality with entity, atomic function or template, higher-order composition, effect class, required authority and disposition.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":968,
          "quote": "| Discovered functionality | Entity | Atomic function or template | Higher-order composition | Effect class | Required authority | Disposition |",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/DESIGN_MODULE.md#DESIGN_MODULE%2F5.%20Ontology-first%20design%2FAtomic%20functions%20and%20higher-order%20composition",
          "start_line":968
        }
      ],
      "key": "atomic_function_family",
      "label": "Atomic function family"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Higher-order composition is governed by an explicit composition and effect algebra. Typical categories are unit or lift, sequential composition, parallel or applicative composition, fold or evaluation, retry or recovery, recursion or fixed point, and projection.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":980,
          "quote": "Typical categories include unit\nor lift, sequential composition, parallel or applicative composition, fold\nor evaluation, retry or recovery, recursion or fixed point, and\nprojection.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/DESIGN_MODULE.md#DESIGN_MODULE%2F5.%20Ontology-first%20design%2FAtomic%20functions%20and%20higher-order%20composition",
          "start_line":977
        }
      ],
      "key": "higher_order_composition",
      "label": "Higher-order composition"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "A Prime semantic atom is an irreducible design relation: an atomic function family, identity, authority transition, lifecycle relation, topology, effect, closure law or composition law that cannot honestly be composed from other accepted atoms with the same meaning. Prime is decided at semantic-design altitude.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1002,
          "quote": "A Prime semantic atom is one irreducible design relation: an atomic\nfunction family, identity, authority transition, lifecycle relation,\ntopology, effect, closure law or composition law that cannot be expressed\nhonestly by composing other accepted atoms while preserving the same\nmeaning",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/DESIGN_MODULE.md#DESIGN_MODULE%2F5.%20Ontology-first%20design%2FPrime%20semantic%20atoms%20and%20admitted%20domain",
          "start_line":998
        }
      ],
      "key": "prime_atom",
      "label": "Prime semantic atom"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Whole-family Prime contraction reviews the entire candidate semantic-atom family and every proposed realisation projection. Recursive contraction reviews individual atoms and then their resulting family; local irreducibility or local Prime-conformance does not establish global Prime.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1028,
          "quote": "Prime review evaluates the complete candidate semantic-atom family and\nevery proposed realisation projection, not only functions, carriers or\neach atom in isolation.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/DESIGN_MODULE.md#DESIGN_MODULE%2F5.%20Ontology-first%20design%2FWhole-family%20Prime%20contraction",
          "start_line":1026
        }
      ],
      "key": "prime_contraction",
      "label": "Whole-family Prime contraction"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Realisation projections include functions, classes, carrier types, top-level schema records and modules. A unit can implement several atoms and an atom can project into several units; physical size, separate naming, typing and independent testability do not establish Prime.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1011,
          "quote": "One atom may project into several realisation\nunits, and one realisation unit may implement several atoms, provided the\nmapping is explicit and introduces no rival meaning or authority.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/DESIGN_MODULE.md#DESIGN_MODULE%2F5.%20Ontology-first%20design%2FPrime%20semantic%20atoms%20and%20admitted%20domain",
          "start_line":1007
        }
      ],
      "key": "realisation_projection",
      "label": "Realisation projection"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The Irreducible Architectural Carrier Set, IACS, is the smallest lawful top-level carrier family needed for a boundary's real functionality and authority flow. Example roles are source truth, admission, execution, yielded or recovery, effect plan and public projection carriers.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1176,
          "quote": "The IACS is the smallest\nset of carriers required to carry the real functionality and authority\nflow of the boundary.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/DESIGN_MODULE.md#DESIGN_MODULE%2F6.%20Prime%20law%2FIrreducible%20architectural%20carrier%20set",
          "start_line":1174
        }
      ],
      "key": "iacs",
      "label": "Irreducible Architectural Carrier Set"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "A Subordinate Payload is detail inside a boundary, not top-level by default. The Promotion Test is the explicit test preceding promotion to a top-level type. Boundary Inflation is promotion of subordinate detail into extra peer types without irreducible need.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1123,
          "quote": "- Subordinate Payload: payload detail that exists inside that boundary but\n  is not top-level by default;\n- Promotion Test: the explicit test a Subordinate Payload passes before it\n  becomes a top-level type; and\n- Boundary Inflation: the defect where subordinate payload detail is\n  promoted into extra peer types without irreducible need.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/DESIGN_MODULE.md#DESIGN_MODULE%2F6.%20Prime%20law",
          "start_line":1118
        }
      ],
      "key": "payload_promotion",
      "label": "Subordinate Payload, Promotion Test and Boundary Inflation"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The three-view design asset contains a structural domain classDiagram, behavioural execution sequenceDiagram and lifecycle stateDiagram-v2. These are fidelity-checked Ontology projections and jointly a sign-off surface for domain identity, authority, behaviour, lifecycle and axiom conformance.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1327,
          "quote": "The asset contains all three views: a Mermaid `classDiagram` domain model;\na Mermaid `sequenceDiagram` execution model; and a Mermaid\n`stateDiagram-v2` lifecycle model.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/DESIGN_MODULE.md#DESIGN_MODULE%2F7.%20Ontology%20and%20three-view%20design%20gate",
          "start_line":1325
        }
      ],
      "key": "three_view_design",
      "label": "Three-view design asset"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Invariant reconstruction uses two distinct inputs: the selected method basis specifies required relations and reasoning law; accepted design supplies concrete invariant values. Its four decisions about an action against one governing relation are preserves, changes, duplicates and violates, mapped to core values for the specific acting-frame admissibility predicate.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1374,
          "quote": "the selected method describes which relations\nare required and how to reason about them; the accepted design supplies\nthe concrete invariant values.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/DESIGN_MODULE.md#DESIGN_MODULE%2F7.%20Ontology%20and%20three-view%20design%20gate%2FInvariant%20reconstruction%20sufficiency%20%28UP-018%29",
          "start_line":1372
        }
      ],
      "key": "invariant_reconstruction",
      "label": "Invariant reconstruction and action decisions"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Cross-view axiom assessment has domain verdicts pass, fail and reasoned not_applicable, or directly recorded core values when no domain verdict is reached. Its predicate is that on the exact Ontology and design basis the design meets the axiom OR the axiom does not apply; non-application is distinct from conformance.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1515,
          "quote": "on the exact\nOntology and design basis, the boundary's design meets the axiom, or the\naxiom does not apply to the boundary",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/DESIGN_MODULE.md#DESIGN_MODULE%2F7.%20Ontology%20and%20three-view%20design%20gate%2FCross-view%20axiom%20evaluation",
          "start_line":1513
        }
      ],
      "key": "axiom_assessment",
      "label": "Cross-view axiom assessment"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Ontology and design verdicts are separate acceptance results owned by the design authority. Each consumes availability and assessment results. accepted is the disposition accept, mapping to satisfied for the candidate's acceptance at its declared design-gate boundary; production of a pack or passing assessments is not that decision.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1559,
          "quote": "The Ontology verdict and the design verdict are each an acceptance\nresult: a decision that consumes the results of assessment, distinct from\nthem.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/DESIGN_MODULE.md#DESIGN_MODULE%2F7.%20Ontology%20and%20three-view%20design%20gate%2FGate%20and%20retrospective%20work",
          "start_line":1557
        }
      ],
      "key": "design_acceptance",
      "label": "Ontology and design acceptance"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The subordinate computational realisation projection exposes abstract data types, identity/key structures, algorithms, algebraic laws, mutation/effect boundaries, selected implementations and domain-to-computational mappings. Material components carry explicit semantic and operational role sets; computational similarity identifies commonisation candidates without deciding semantic identity, ownership or authority.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1651,
          "quote": "the design projects the\ndomain design into its computational realisation: abstract data types,\nidentity and key structures, algorithms, algebraic laws, mutation and\neffect boundaries, selected implementations, and domain-to-computational\nmappings.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/DESIGN_MODULE.md#DESIGN_MODULE%2F7.%20Ontology%20and%20three-view%20design%20gate%2FComputational%20realisation%20projection%20%28UP-019%29",
          "start_line":1647
        }
      ],
      "key": "computational_projection",
      "label": "Computational realisation projection"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Recommended module roles are carrier (typed immutable inputs, outputs, contracts, transitions, records or envelopes), semantic kernel (main carrier transforms without effects), effect shell (event emission, files, network, subprocesses, persistence or operator delivery), projection (read models/reports from authoritative truth), binding/adapter (external/internal conversion without inventing law), and constructor/materialisation (generated/built artifacts from admitted carriers and explicit plans). Boundaries support finite-context reasoning, ownership/consumption/emission/proof review and visibility of recurrence.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1752,
          "quote": "modules should be\nclassified by role (P0, P6; rc.2 #6-design-module-taxonomy). Recommended\nroles are:",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/DESIGN_MODULE.md#DESIGN_MODULE%2F9.%20Module%20taxonomy",
          "start_line":1750
        }
      ],
      "key": "module_roles",
      "label": "Module taxonomy"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Product semantic functions return Product domain outcomes; evaluations consuming those outcomes return their own method core results. These result domains remain distinct even when explicit closed outcome carriers are preferred.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1818,
          "quote": "The outcome values a\nsemantic function returns are the Product's domain values, not results of\nthis method; an evaluation that consumes them returns its own core value",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/DESIGN_MODULE.md#DESIGN_MODULE%2F10.%20Realisation%20rules%2FTotality",
          "start_line":1816
        }
      ],
      "key": "semantic_function_outcomes",
      "label": "Product outcomes and method results"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Governance surfaces describe state, boundary and admissible routes; strategy surfaces prescribe how another actor should work. Lawful governance publications include current state, preserved structure, unmet pressure or unresolved identities, route eligibility, lawful edit/proof frontier, admitted policy identity and prior-turn/run continuity.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1884,
          "quote": "Governance surfaces describe the state, boundary\nand admissible routes; strategy surfaces prescribe how another actor\nshould do the work.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/DESIGN_MODULE.md#DESIGN_MODULE%2F10.%20Realisation%20rules%2FGovernance%20and%20strategy%20separation",
          "start_line":1882
        }
      ],
      "key": "governance_surface",
      "label": "Governance and strategy surfaces"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "A recurring realisation pattern across more than one processed boundary is a commonisation candidate. Such candidates are usually not Product truth, authority-neutral, reused across two or more tickets or module boundaries and consumable via local adapters without semantic ownership change. Examples include expectation derivation, nested contract/policy carriers, proof fixtures, effect request/plan shapes and constructors that do not own semantic law.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1933,
          "quote": "When the same realisation pattern appears across more than one processed\nboundary, review treats that pattern as a commonisation candidate rather\nthan as another purely local clean-up",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/DESIGN_MODULE.md#DESIGN_MODULE%2F10.%20Realisation%20rules%2FRecurrence%20extraction",
          "start_line":1931
        }
      ],
      "key": "recurrence_candidate",
      "label": "Recurrence and commonisation candidate"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Post-ticket design review checks the causal closure of the exact declared claim against governing requirements, accepted Ontology, Prime atoms, IACS, views, target modules, implementation and module-owned proof. It is bounded to the claim rather than the whole Product or construction history.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":2003,
          "quote": "The complete selected function is the causal closure of the declared\nclaim, not the whole Product or the whole history of its construction.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/DESIGN_MODULE.md#DESIGN_MODULE%2F10.%20Realisation%20rules%2FPost-ticket%20design%20review",
          "start_line":2002
        }
      ],
      "key": "bounded_design_review",
      "label": "Bounded post-ticket design review"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The functional realisation checklist is a language-agnostic review surface for claims of functional bias, pure transforms, immutable carriers, single-owner truth or low coupling. It covers module, carrier and data-entity design and realisation review, with a compact hard gate for new or materially changed boundaries.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":2249,
          "quote": "When a project claims functional bias, pure transforms, immutable\ncarriers, single-owner truth or low-coupling realisation, review\nexplicitly checks",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/DESIGN_MODULE.md#DESIGN_MODULE%2F11.%20Review%2FFunctional%20realisation%20checklist",
          "start_line":2246
        }
      ],
      "key": "functional_review",
      "label": "Functional realisation checklist"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "Source dispositions are authored provenance and conservation claims about predecessor rc.2 DESIGN_MODULE_METHOD rows and one IDENTITY_METHOD row. Anchors default to the predecessor design member, loci to the receiving file unless named otherwise. A route is provenance only, and a conserved row is claimed to be restated at its receiving locus.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":2511,
          "quote": "Every conserved row\nis restated in the receiving member's own text; a route is provenance\nonly.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/DESIGN_MODULE.md#DESIGN_MODULE%2F13.%20Source%20dispositions",
          "start_line":2509
        }
      ],
      "key": "source_disposition_register",
      "label": "Source disposition register"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "The theoretical Markov-object framing treats a stable self-bounding pattern as a candidate identity-bearing cut whose effective blanket separates identity-bearing from contextual support. It analogises IACS to the smallest lawful identity-bearing cut, payload promotion to independent boundary authority, inflation to mistaking fragments for peers, and strong typing to keeping the cut explicit. This framing is justification, not a prerequisite or scope expansion.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":1730,
          "quote": "It treats a Markov object\nas a candidate identity-bearing cut: a stable self-bounding pattern whose\neffective blanket separates what is load-bearing for identity from what is\nload-bearing for context.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/DESIGN_MODULE.md#DESIGN_MODULE%2F8.%20Theoretical%20framing",
          "start_line":1727
        }
      ],
      "key": "markov_object_framing",
      "label": "Optional Markov-object framing"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "A consumer-bound ABG-role realisation under the graph-execution profile may supply default identity generators, validators and normalisers for its runtime surfaces and default ODD surface kinds used by default F_D helpers.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":2486,
          "quote": "A consumer-bound realisation of the ABG role under the graph-execution\nprofile may publish default identity generators, validators and\nnormalisers for its own runtime surfaces and for the default ODD surface\nkinds used by default `F_D` helpers",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/DESIGN_MODULE.md#DESIGN_MODULE%2F12.%20Adoption%20and%20relations%2FIdentity%20defaults%20from%20a%20bound%20runtime%20realisation",
          "start_line":2483
        }
      ],
      "key": "bound_runtime_identity_defaults",
      "label": "Bound-runtime identity defaults"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "EVALUATION is described as a Tier 1 member owning claim justification and acceptance, evidence, independent assessment, review results, realisation conformance and equivalence, frame/method qualification and actual-use evidence of adequate attention. It consumes FOUNDATIONS core results and defines no result values of its own.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":9,
          "quote": "Tier 1. Owns how claims are justified and accepted: claims, evidence,\nindependent assessment, review results, acceptance, conformance of\nrealisations and the equivalence ladder, the qualification of frames and\nof the method itself, and actual-use evidence of adequate attention.\nConsumes [FOUNDATIONS](FOUNDATIONS.md), whose\n[Results](FOUNDATIONS.md#results) own the core result values this member\nuses; it defines no result values of its own.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/EVALUATION.md#EVALUATION",
          "start_line":3
        }
      ],
      "key": "evaluation_member",
      "label": "EVALUATION member"
    },
    {
      "conditions": [],
      "definition": "A load-bearing claim specifies exact subject, claimed property, governing basis, witness-to-claim relation, excluded nearest weaker property and falsifier before selecting a witness. Without this specification the activity is evidence discovery, not proof.",
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":16,
          "quote": "A load-bearing claim names its exact subject, the property claimed, the\ngoverning basis, the relation between witness and claim, the nearest weaker\nproperty it excludes, and its falsifier, before a witness is selected.\nWithout these it is evidence discovery, not proof.",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/EVALUATION.md#EVALUATION%2F1.%20Claims",
          "start_line":13
        }
      ],
      "key": "load_bearing_claim",
      "label": "Load-bearing claim"
    }
  ],
  "constraints": [
    {
      "applies_to_keys": ["atomic_function_family", "higher_order_composition"],
      "conditions": ["Higher-order algebra declarations apply where the boundary has higher-order behaviour."],
      "exceptions": [],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":974,
          "quote": "Every row is one of: derived through an admitted atomic function; derived\nthrough a declared higher-order composition; deferred with an owner and\nre-entry condition; excluded by current product authority; or unresolved\nas a named gap",
          "section_id": "repo://stdo-v3-generation-rc9/claude/c2cfa9b908d1722ae3f26c32fa4ece691083bdc3/source/standards/DESIGN_MODULE.md#DESIGN_MODULE%2F5.%20Ontology-first%20design%2FAtomic%20functions%20and%20higher-order%20composition",
          "start_line":971
        }
      ],
      "key": "function_derivation_and_algebra",
      "statement": "Derive the smallest parameterised atomic families making lifecycles logically complete. Variation only in entity kind, projection kind, transition kind, policy or subordinate payload should normally be a typed parameter or closed variant, not a peer function. The minimum derivation matrix uses all seven declared columns. Every functionality row is derived through an admitted atomic function, derived through declared composition, deferred with owner and re-entry condition, excluded by current Product authority, or unresolved as a named gap. For higher-order behaviour name the governing composition/effect algebra and applicable identity, closure, associativity, cardinality, effect and authority-conservation laws explicitly rather than hiding them in controller flow. No private helper, loop, adapter or endpoint may become an undeclared constructor.",
      "uncertainty": ""
    },
    {
      "applies_to_keys": ["ontology", "realisation_projection", "design_acceptance"],
      "conditions": ["Iterative discovery or retained implementation crosses an affected unresolved, contradictory or materially non-equivalent relation."],
      "exceptions": ["Before design acceptance, a disposable spike may supply evidence when co_evolution_admissible(B) does not hold."],
      "grounding": [
        {
          "end_line":993,
          "quote": "Composition, effects and implementation may be discovered iteratively only\nwhen `co_evolution_admissible(B)` holds. Otherwise only a disposable spike\nmay supply evidence across the affected unresolved, contradictory or\nmaterially non-equivalent relation before its design is accepted. Every\nretained result is reconciled into accepted design before promotion or\ndesign-method closure.",
